_nf_new(_ATD, "Hon. Aaron Tufts of Dudley (1770&ndash;1844)", "30 Jan. 1770 &ndash; 17 Oct. 1844",
  "Charlestown (Somerville), Mass.; Dudley, Mass.", "1770", "1844", "19th Century",
  "Justice of the peace and woolen manufacturer of Tuftsville, Dudley; son of Aaron and Mary (Stone) Tufts",
  "<p>Aaron Tufts of Dudley, styled Hon. and sometimes Dr. in later compilations, was a woolen manufacturer and a justice of the peace. He was one of the original corporators of the Merino Wool Factory, with Learned Corbin, Jeptha Bacon, Phineas Bemis and William Learned, incorporated 13 February 1812 (" + _HURD_WORC + "). In 1816 he and partners, among them John R. Jewett, Mayo Pratt, Harvey Conant and William Robinson, built a woolen mill in the north part of the town, on a privilege supplied from Gore and Baker Ponds, a place later called Ram&rsquo;s Horn or Tufts Village. A stone mill replaced the earlier frame building in 1827; in 1832 it was reported to have made 40,000 yards of broadcloth (Massachusetts Historical Commission, Reconnaissance Survey Town Report: Dudley, 1984).</p>"
  "<p>The family record gives his birth as 30 January 1770 at Charlestown, a son of Aaron Tufts (1739&ndash;1773) and Mary (Stone) Tufts, his marriage to Sarah (Sally) Barker at Worcester on 23 May 1790, and his death at Dudley on 17 October 1844 (" + _KV1 + ", p.&nbsp;112, no. 56-1, citing the Massachusetts vital records, vol. 9, p. 124). It notes service in the War of 1812, as a guard at the State Arsenal, and that he was an original stockholder of the Manufacturers&rsquo; and Merchants&rsquo; Bank in Boston in 1814, before he built up the community called Tuftsville. Sarah died at Dudley on 2 March 1842 (Dudley vital records, p. 283, as cited there). One son, George A., is recorded.</p>"
  "<p>He is not " + PL("aaron-tufts", "Aaron Tufts of Medford (1761&ndash;1781)") + ", who died long before this mill was built, and not " + PL("aaron-tufts-1793", "Aaron Tufts (1793&ndash;1881)") + " of Pavilion, New York.</p>",
  "<p>Civil office: justice of the peace for Worcester County (the 1825 writ on this page). The family record notes service in the War of 1812 as a guard at the State Arsenal (" + _KV1 + ", p.&nbsp;112). No Revolutionary service is expected; he was a child in 1775.</p>",
  "<p>Parents: Aaron Tufts (16 Dec. 1739 &ndash; 29 Oct. 1773) of Charlestown, and Mary Stone (22 Nov. 1747 &ndash; 22 Oct. 1824), daughter of Lieut. Moses and Hannah (Tainter) Stone of Watertown; she married, second, Capt. William Lyon of Woodstock, Conn. (" + _KV1 + ", no. 18-6 and p.&nbsp;112). Wife: Sarah Barker. Child: George A.</p>",
  "<strong>Death year.</strong> The family record, citing Massachusetts vital records, vol. 9, p. 124, gives 17 October 1844. Hurd&rsquo;s 1889 county history says that in 1843, &ldquo;upon the death of Mr. Tufts,&rdquo; John R. Jewett succeeded to the mill; that 1843 date has not been reconciled with the vital-record year. <strong>Birth and parentage</strong> are from <em>Tufts Kinsmen</em>, Vol.&nbsp;I, p.&nbsp;112, and have not been checked against the Charlestown and Watertown volumes in this pass. The printed page of Hurd&rsquo;s Dudley chapter has not been confirmed. Note: Pending further work.",
  "<ul><li><strong>Sarah (Sally) Barker</strong> (25 Sept. 1767 &ndash; 2 Mar. 1842), daughter of William and Mary (Yale/Gilbert) Barker of Worcester; married 23 May 1790 at Worcester; died at Dudley (" + _KV1 + ", p.&nbsp;112; Dudley VR, p. 283, as cited there).</li></ul>",
  [("aaron-tufts", "a different man: Aaron Tufts of Medford (1761&ndash;1781)", "a different man: Hon. Aaron Tufts of Dudley (1770&ndash;1844)"),
   ("aaron-tufts-1793", "a different man: Aaron Tufts (1793&ndash;1881) of Pavilion, N.Y.", "a different man: Hon. Aaron Tufts of Dudley (1770&ndash;1844)")])

